# Post-processing scripts

R scripts producing the figures in Corbier et al., *Beyond Ownership: Lifestyle, infrastructures, and distributional challenges for Product-Service Systems*.

These read **model output**, not the model itself. Run CIRCEE first (see the main README), then point these scripts at the resulting files.

---

## Inputs

| Input | Produced by |
| --- | --- |
| `<lifestyle>_AE_<scenario>.csv` — one per run, wide format (`Row`, `Y2018`, `Y2019`, …) | `src/CIRCEE_RunFile.m` |
| `NoModifiers_AE.csv` — the reference run | `src/CIRCEE_RunFile.m`, all modifiers at zero |
| `welfare_CEV_lifetime.csv` and the other welfare CSVs | `src/CIRCEE_WelfarePostProcess.m` |
| `shocks_final_<lifestyle>_<scenario>.csv` — long format (`Variable`; `Region`; `Year`; `Value`) | the coupled pipeline, `scripts/run.sh` |

### What the filenames encode

Two dimensions vary across runs: the infrastructures configurations (the substitution elasticity path) and the behavioural modifiers (the behavioural configuration). Filenames encode both, and `Baseline` refers to the infrastructures, not to the
absence of behaviour.

| File | Infrastructures | Behavioural modifiers |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `NoModifiers_AE.csv` | BAU | all zero — the reference run |
| `<behavioural>_AE_Baseline.csv` | BAU | as set by the behavioural configuration |
| `<behavioural>_AE_Progressive.csv` | Progressive | as set by the behavioural configuration |
| `<behavioural>_AE_Regressive.csv` | Regressive | as set by the behavioural configuration |

`AE` denotes anticipation errors. `<behavioural>` is one of `ecoactive_ecoactive`, `affordability_affordability`, `ecoactive_affordability`, `affordability_ecoactive` — sharing driver followed by sufficiency driver.

Twelve scenario runs are expected (four behavioural configurations × three infrastructures), plus the reference run.

**All figures report changes relative to `NoModifiers_AE.csv`**: the BAU infrastructures with no behavioural change (no lfiestyle heterogeneity).

### Code tokens vs. paper labels

| Code token | Paper label |
| --- | --- |
| `Baseline` | BAU |
| `Progressive` | Progressive |
| `Regressive` | Regressive |
| `NoModifiers` | reference run |
| `lowcarbon` | Higher income |
| `cautious` | Medium income |
| `constrained` | Lower income |

Lifestyle group names describe archetypes from Pettifor et al. (2023) rather than income deciles; the income ordering is a property of the groups, not their definition.

### Setting the paths

Set the input and output directories at the top of each script:

```r
levels_dir <- "path/to/CIRCEE_output_levels"
out_dir    <- "path/to/figures"
```

---

## Main figures

| Script | Produces |
| --- | --- |
| `Figure_2.R` | Behavioural engagement: 2050 heatmap, Refuse and Rethink trajectories |
| `Figure_3.R` | Environmental footprints, and footprint change by consumption good |
| `Figure_4.R` | Material flows, waste flows, cumulative CO2 |
| `Figure_5.R` | Energy-service access ratios, lifetime CEV by group, trilemma panel |

## Supplementary figures

| Script | Produces | Needs first |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `SI/Carbon_and_waste_footprint_SI.R` | Footprint change by good, all four behavioural configurations | `Figure_3.R` |
| `SI/TrilemmaWaste_SI.R` | Trilemma panel with the waste footprint on the vertical axis | `Figure_5.R` |
| `SI/Prices_SI.R` | Price divergence between home production and PSS energy services | — |
| `SI/Userates_SI.R` | Use rates of owned and PSS energy-using goods | — |
| `SI/Energy_SI.R` | Exogenous WITCH/SSP2 inputs: efficiencies, emission factors, price growth | `scen_meta`, from any main script |
| `SI/Sensitivitybounds_SI.R` | Sensitivity of the behavioural response to the substitution elasticity | — |

`Sensitivitybounds_SI.R` carries its values inline rather than reading a file, so it runs standalone.

---

## Order of execution

Three SI scripts reuse objects built by other scripts. Source those first, **in a clean session**:

```r
rm(list = ls())
source("Figure_3.R")
source("SI/Carbon_and_waste_footprint_SI.R")

rm(list = ls())
source("Figure_5.R")
source("SI/TrilemmaWaste_SI.R")
```

Clearing the workspace matters. These scripts share object names (`group_levels`, `behavioural_labels`, `shapes_sc`, `scen_meta` and others), so a stale copy left from a previous run can silently mismatch a factor level. `ggplot` then drops the affected rows with a "Removed *n* rows containing missing values" warning rather than an error, and the figure renders with points missing.

---

## Requirements

R >= 4.0 with:

```r
install.packages(c("tidyverse", "patchwork", "ggh4x", "ggrepel", "ggnewscale",
                   "cowplot", "gridExtra", "gtable", "readxl", "scales"))
```

`grid` ships with R. `dplyr`, `tidyr`, `purrr`, `stringr`, `ggplot2` and `readr`
come with `tidyverse`.

---

## Conventions

* Footprint changes are expressed as a percentage of the reference-run footprint, except where a panel's axis label states otherwise.
* Inequality measures are computed on per-household quantities; with three lifestyle groups, decile-based measures are not well defined (see the main README's welfare section).
